      <h2 id="legal">Are tiny homes legal in Texas?</h2>
      <p>Yes. Texas does not ban tiny homes, and many Texans live in them full time. What Texas does not have is a dedicated statewide tiny house statute. Instead, a tiny home is regulated by whatever category it falls into — a single-family dwelling, an accessory dwelling unit, a recreational vehicle, a park model RV, a manufactured home or a modular (industrialized) home — and by the local government that controls the land it sits on.</p>
      <p>That is why two people asking "are tiny homes legal in Texas?" can get opposite answers. A 350-square-foot home on a slab in one suburb may sail through permitting, while the same home on wheels parked in a backyard a few miles away can violate city code. The answer always starts with two questions: <strong>what kind of tiny home is it</strong>, and <strong>who has jurisdiction over the land</strong>?</p>

      <h2 id="types">How Texas classifies a tiny home</h2>
      <p>The way your home is built decides which code applies, who inspects it, and how it can be financed and insured.</p>
      <div class="table-wrap"><table>
        <thead><tr><th>Home type</th><th>Typically regulated as</th><th>Code / standard</th><th>Who oversees it</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Tiny house on a foundation</td><td>Single-family dwelling or ADU</td><td>International Residential Code (IRC), often with Appendix Q for homes ≤ 400 sq ft</td><td>City building department; zoning rules</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Tiny house on wheels (THOW)</td><td>Recreational vehicle</td><td>NFPA 1192 (successor to ANSI A119.2) or ANSI A119.5; RVIA or NOAH certification</td><td>Titled and registered as a vehicle/trailer; local parking and RV rules</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Park model RV</td><td>Recreational vehicle</td><td>ANSI A119.5; under 400 sq ft in set-up mode</td><td>RV park rules; local zoning for placement</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Manufactured (HUD-code) home</td><td>Manufactured housing</td><td>Federal HUD code</td><td>Texas Department of Housing and Community Affairs (TDHCA) Manufactured Housing Division</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Modular home</td><td>Industrialized housing</td><td>Built to the local residential code in a factory</td><td>Texas Industrialized Housing and Buildings program (TDLR) plus local permits</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table></div>

      <h3>Tiny houses on a foundation and IRC Appendix Q</h3>
      <p>The International Residential Code is the building code most Texas cities adopt for houses. Starting with the 2018 edition, the IRC includes an optional appendix for tiny houses — Appendix Q, renumbered Appendix AQ in the 2021 edition — for dwellings of 400 square feet or less, not counting lofts. It relaxes rules that make very small homes hard to build, such as minimum ceiling heights in lofts, and allows ladders or alternating-tread stairs to reach sleeping lofts, while keeping requirements like emergency escape openings and smoke alarms.</p>
      <p>The catch: <strong>appendices only apply if your city adopts them.</strong> Some Texas cities have adopted Appendix Q/AQ; many have not. If your city has not, a tiny house can still be permitted — it simply has to meet the standard IRC requirements, which a good designer can plan for from day one.</p>

      <h3>Tiny houses on wheels and park models</h3>
      <p>A tiny house on wheels is generally treated as a recreational vehicle, not a house. That means it is titled and registered like a trailer, and certification matters: a THOW built and inspected to an RV standard (through RVIA membership or a third-party inspector such as NOAH) is far easier to insure, finance and place in an RV park. Uncertified homes can be hard to insure and are often refused by parks.</p>
      <p>Because they are RVs, many Texas cities restrict living full time in a THOW on a residential lot, or limit how long one can be occupied outside an RV park. Rural land outside city limits, RV parks and tiny home communities are the most common long-term homes for THOWs and park models. Moving a home wider than 8 feet 6 inches or taller than the legal height limit typically requires an oversize permit from the Texas Department of Motor Vehicles.</p>

      <h2 id="local">City limits, ETJ and county land</h2>
      <p>The second big question is who controls your land.</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Inside city limits:</strong> cities can zone land and adopt building codes. Expect a building permit, plan review and inspections, plus zoning rules on minimum house size, setbacks, lot coverage, parking and whether a second dwelling (ADU) is allowed. Many cities also restrict RV living on residential lots.</li>
        <li><strong>Extraterritorial jurisdiction (ETJ):</strong> the band of land just outside many Texas cities. Cities can apply subdivision and platting rules in the ETJ but generally cannot zone it. State law changes in 2023 also gave many landowners a path to petition out of a city's ETJ, so check your property's current status with the city.</li>
        <li><strong>Unincorporated county land:</strong> Texas counties have very limited zoning power, so there is usually no county zoning or general building permit for a house. But county-level rules still apply — on-site sewage facility (septic) permits under Texas Commission on Environmental Quality rules, floodplain development permits, 911 addressing, driveway or culvert permits, and subdivision rules if land is being divided.</li>
      </ul>
      <div class="callout"><p><strong>Don't confuse "no zoning" with "no rules."</strong> Houston famously has no traditional zoning, but deed restrictions, subdivision rules and building codes still apply. Rural land with no county zoning can still carry deed restrictions that ban mobile homes, RVs or homes under a minimum size.</p></div>

      <h2 id="adu">ADUs: the easiest path inside cities</h2>
      <p>If you want a tiny home in a backyard inside a city, it will usually be permitted as an accessory dwelling unit. ADU rules vary widely: some cities allow them by right in most residential zones, others require a special exception, and most set limits on size relative to the main house, height, setbacks, parking and sometimes owner occupancy. Austin, for example, broadened what can be built on many single-family lots through its HOME code amendments, while other cities still treat ADUs case by case. See our <a href="/adu-builders-texas/">ADU builders page</a> and the <a href="/service-areas/">city pages</a> for local notes — and always confirm current code, because ADU rules across Texas are actively changing.</p>

      <h2 id="hoa">HOAs and deed restrictions</h2>
      <p>Even where a city says yes, a homeowners association or recorded deed restriction can say no. Common restrictions include minimum square footage for any dwelling, bans on manufactured homes or RVs, limits on accessory structures and architectural approval requirements. Under Texas law these restrictions are generally enforceable, so read the deed restrictions and HOA covenants for any property <em>before</em> you buy it. A title company can provide copies.</p>

      <h2 id="utilities">Utilities, septic and water</h2>
      <p>Your tiny home needs a legal way to handle wastewater, water and power. In town, that means connecting to city water and sewer, with tap and impact fees that can be significant. In the country, you will likely need a septic system permitted and designed to TCEQ rules (most counties or local authorities issue the permit and require a site evaluation), plus a well, rainwater collection or a water co-op connection. Composting toilets and off-grid systems are possible in some rural areas, but you still need an approved way to handle greywater. Budget for this early — see our <a href="/guides/cost-to-build-a-tiny-house-in-texas/">tiny house cost guide</a>.</p>

      <h2 id="checklist">How to check your property: permit checklist</h2>
      <p>Work through this checklist before you buy land or sign a build contract. Tick each item as you go.</p>
      <div class="permit-check" data-checklist>
        <h3>Texas tiny home permit checklist</h3>
        <label><input type="checkbox"> Confirm whether the property is inside city limits, in a city's ETJ, or in unincorporated county land.</label>
        <label><input type="checkbox"> Decide your home type: foundation (IRC), tiny home on wheels, park model, or modular/manufactured.</label>
        <label><input type="checkbox"> Look up the zoning district and whether a primary tiny home or an ADU is allowed there.</label>
        <label><input type="checkbox"> Ask the building department which IRC edition they use and whether they adopted Appendix Q/AQ.</label>
        <label><input type="checkbox"> Check minimum dwelling size, setbacks, height, lot coverage and parking requirements.</label>
        <label><input type="checkbox"> Read the deed restrictions and HOA covenants for size minimums and RV or manufactured home bans.</label>
        <label><input type="checkbox"> Check the FEMA flood map and whether a floodplain development permit is required.</label>
        <label><input type="checkbox"> Confirm water and sewer availability, or get a septic site evaluation and well/water plan.</label>
        <label><input type="checkbox"> Confirm electric service availability and the cost to extend power to the site.</label>
        <label><input type="checkbox"> For a THOW or park model: confirm RVIA/NOAH certification and where it can legally be occupied.</label>
        <label><input type="checkbox"> Get 911 addressing and driveway/culvert permits if required.</label>
        <label><input type="checkbox"> Get written confirmation from the permitting office before you commit.</label>
        <p data-checklist-count></p>
      </div>
      <p>The feasibility study in every Cowboy Tiny Homes design package covers these items for your exact address, so you know what you can build before you pay for full drawings.</p>

      <h2 id="mistakes">Common mistakes to avoid</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Buying land first, checking rules later.</strong> Deed restrictions and septic feasibility are the two most common deal-breakers.</li>
        <li><strong>Buying an uncertified THOW</strong> and discovering no insurer, lender or RV park will accept it.</li>
        <li><strong>Assuming "rural" means "anything goes."</strong> Septic permits, floodplain rules and deed restrictions still apply.</li>
        <li><strong>Building a THOW to live in a city backyard</strong> where RV occupancy is not allowed; an ADU on a foundation may be the legal route.</li>
        <li><strong>Skipping written confirmation.</strong> Verbal answers at a permit counter are not a guarantee — get it in writing.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>Rules change, and this guide is general information, not legal advice. When in doubt, call your local building department, or ask Cowboy Tiny Homes to run the feasibility check for you.</p>
